#!/bin/sh
# CrowAgent pre-push gate (see /ENGINEERING-STANDARDS.md §3).
#
# THIS GATE IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY AS OF 2026-09-01. It ran `npm test`, the jest
# suite, and that suite tests a tree nobody serves.
#
# MEASURED, not assumed. jest.config.js declares a coverage floor over exactly
# four files: scripts.js, cookie-banner.js, js/cookie-banner.js and
# service-worker.js. The live site is built from astro/ and ships as astro/dist.
# Across all 46 built pages, the count of references to any of those four files
# is ZERO. The negative result was available and would have been obvious: a
# nonzero count for any one of them would have kept the gate. There was none.
#
# So every push paid a jest run with coverage instrumentation to protect code
# that the site stopped loading on 2026-08-05, when the Cloudflare Pages deploy
# source moved to astro/dist. The suite itself still passes and is untouched. It
# is simply not a gate on anything that ships, and a gate that reaches nothing is
# worse than no gate, because it is believed.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY GUARDS A PUSH NOW: `npm run build` inside astro/, which runs the
# full gate chain (facts, English, links, SEO and content parity, design system,
# palette roles, motion, CSP, render, plus the browser gates). Run it before you
# push. A push to main IS a production Cloudflare deploy, so it is worth the time.
#
# NOT WIRED IN HERE ON PURPOSE. Putting that chain in this hook would turn a free
# local convenience into a several-minute block on every push, and wiring a gate
# is an owner decision under RULE 0-G, not a side effect of removing a stale one.
# The cheapest useful candidate, if the owner wants one, is
# `node astro/scripts/check-facts.js` at about 135 ms, because a check-facts
# violation FAILS the Cloudflare deploy and is the one class of error that costs
# a real production build to discover.
#
# To run the old suite by hand: npm test
exit 0
